@{
    Layout = "~/Views/Shared/_Layout.cshtml";
    ViewBag.Title = "Part12 - Understanding grid render mechanism";
}

<div class="col-sm-12">
    <h4>1) Grid components</h4>
    <p>There are six parts and two scrolls to render a typical grid, grid will render each part of those things, then compute the location of each part. Let’s see the functionality of each part.</p>

    <img width="100%" src="~/Content/Images/Part12-GridExplain.png" />

    <table class="table">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>
                    Part
                </th>
                <th>
                    Description
                </th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>

            <tr>
                <td>
                    Free header (FH)
                </td>
                <td>
                    This part will fixed in screen, it cannot move everywhere when we scroll, this<br>
                    part will appear when we use option “numberOfFrozenColumn”, and set it greater than 0.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>
                    Header (H)
                </td>
                <td>
                    This part will always stay in top at grid when we scroll SY, but it will move when we scroll SX
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>
                    Free body (FB)
                </td>
                <td>
                    This part will always stay in left, and only scroll when we scroll SY
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>
                    Body (B)
                </td>
                <td>
                    This part can scroll by SX and SY
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>
                    Free footer (FF)
                </td>
                <td>
                    Like FH, this part will always stay in bottom of grid.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>
                    Footer (F)
                </td>
                <td>
                    Like H, thí part will always stay in bottom of grid, but it will move when we scroll SX
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>
                    Scroll X (SX)
                </td>
                <td>
                    This part will handle grid move to left or right when we scroll this, in engine,<br>
                    we have the code to move H, B, F synchronize with scroll, when
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>
                    Scroll Y (SY)
                </td>
                <td>
                    This part will handle grid move from top to bottom or vice versa, just move FB and B synchronize.
                </td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>So you have a simple understand about Fanex Grid components. Let’s suppose that we scroll SY to bottom, so what will happen in grid?</p>

    <h4>2) Mechanism</h4>
    <p>From user view, they will see all grid body will move to the bottom, but let’s think about what happen behind the scene :D . Grid engine will move scroll of B and FB simultaneously, by this way, user will feel the gird move like a single gird body.</p>
    <p>Like SY, SX have same mechanics, when SX move from the left to the right, grid engine will move scroll of H, B, F from the left to the right. User will see a single block move in the grid. Let see the image below, and map what i told before.</p>

    <img width="100%" src="~/Content/Images/Part12-GridExplain2.png" />

    <p>Ok, we can understand the gird mechanics handle when user interact, and now we will map this mechanics to the rendered grid.</p>

    <img width="100%" src="~/Content/Images/Part12-GridExplain3.png" />

    <p>Finally, we have an overview of rendering mechanics of Fanex.Grid. Now we can enjoy grid examples. :D</p>
</div>
